async #getHighestCount(): Promise<number> {
    const result = await this.#emailModel.findPage({
        filter: `created_at:<=${new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0]}`,
        order: 'csd_email_count DESC',
        limit: 1
    });

// For high volume senders (400k+), cap the increase at 20% or 75k absolute
if (lastCount >= WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.threshold) {
    const scaledIncrease = Math.ceil(lastCount * WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.maxScale);
    const absoluteIncrease = lastCount + WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.highVolume.maxAbsoluteIncrease;
    return Math.min(scaledIncrease, absoluteIncrease);
}

for (const threshold of WARMUP_SCALING_TABLE.thresholds.sort((a, b) => a.limit - b.limit)) {
    if (lastCount < threshold.limit) {
        return Math.ceil(lastCount * threshold.scale);
    }
}

findings.body:
The filter changed from `created_at:<today` to `created_at:<=today`. The comment at line 98 still says "excluding today", and the PR description does not explain the change. On a date-only value, `<=` is almost the same as `<`, but it is still a semantic change, and the code and comment now disagree.

The unit test at line 181 asserts `callArgs.filter.includes(`created_at:<${today}`)`. The filter now contains `created_at:<=`, so the substring check fails.

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="100-105"}

::suggestion{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="102-102"}
filter: `created_at:<${new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0]}`,

If the inclusive comparison is intended, change the comment and the unit test together.

For lastCount ≥ 375k, `x + 75000 ≤ 1.2 × x`, so `Math.min(scaledIncrease, absoluteIncrease)` always returns the +75k value. For every input the branch can actually reach (≥ 400k), the 1.2× term never wins. The doc table at lines 30-41 ("min(1.2×, +75k) cap") and the comment at line 124 imply both limits matter, which is misleading.

::code{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="124-129"}

If the intent is a flat +75k cap, drop `maxScale` and `scaledIncrease`. If the 1.2× term should matter, the cap or threshold needs to be redesigned.

The final `return` in `#getTargetLimit` is documented as unreachable, which is true since the loop covers everything below 400k and the branch above it covers the rest. If that assumption ever breaks (for example, if a threshold is removed), the fallback silently applies 1.2× through `highVolume.maxScale`, which is the wrong multiplier for that range. A value-coupled fallback like this hides config mistakes.

::code{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/email-service/DomainWarmingService.ts" lines="131-138"}

Throwing an error or returning the last tier's scale makes a broken config obvious.

The helper now computes `baseDate` once, when the `describe` callback runs (module load), not per test. It also switches the fake clock to `shouldAdvanceTime: true`, so the clock keeps moving with real time between sends. The old helper was pinned per call. The comment "Uses a fixed base date to ensure consistent day progression" describes the opposite of the new behaviour (the base is fixed, but the clock is not). Suites that run across midnight, or that take long enough for the real clock to move, can now see a different day than the one `setDay` set.

::code{file="ghost/core/test/integration/services/email-service/domain-warming.test.js" lines="54-66"}

The `shouldAdvanceTime` change is not needed for the limit math in this PR. Drop it, and compute the base date inside `setDay` to restore the per-call behaviour.

The `getExpectedScale` helper mirrors the production table inside the test. That duplicates the config, so the two can drift. It also disagrees with the implementation at a boundary. The helper maps `count <= 1000` to 1.25×, but the production code sends exactly 1000 into the 1.5× tier. The helper only runs for small day-by-day counts in this test, so the mismatch is latent, but it reinforces the boundary confusion from the unit-test finding.

::code{file="ghost/core/test/integration/services/email-service/domain-warming.test.js" lines="290-301"}

Assert explicit expected values per day instead, the way the multi-day test already does (200, 250, 313).
